{
 "chapterTitle": "Electricity",
 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "Electrical Units",
   "spec": "2.1",
   "cards": [
    {
     "tag": "2.1 Units",
     "q": [
      "What is the unit of electric CURRENT?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "Ampere (A)",
      "→ measured by: Ammeter",
      "→ in SERIES with circuit"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.1 Units",
     "q": [
      "What is the unit of VOLTAGE?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "Volt (V)",
      "→ measured by: Voltmeter",
      "→ in PARALLEL with component"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.1 Units",
     "q": [
      "What is the unit of RESISTANCE?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "Ohm (Ω)",
      "→ R = V ÷ I",
      "→ measured by: Ohmmeter"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.1 Units",
     "q": [
      "What is the unit of CHARGE?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "Coulomb (C)",
      "→ Q = I × t",
      "→ 1A = 1C per second"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.1 Units",
     "q": [
      "What is the unit of POWER?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "Watt (W)",
      "→ P = IV",
      "→ 1W = 1J per second"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.1 Units",
     "q": [
      "What is the unit of ENERGY?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "Joule (J)",
      "→ E = Pt",
      "→ Also used: kWh"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.1 / 2.20",
     "q": [
      "What does 1 Volt mean?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "1 V = 1 Joule per Coulomb",
      "→ 1 V = 1 J/C",
      "→ Energy per unit charge"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.1 Units",
     "q": [
      "How many joules is 1 kilowatt-hour (kWh)?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "1 kWh = 3,600,000 J",
      "= 3.6 MJ",
      "→ Used on electricity bills"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Mains Electricity",
   "spec": "2.2, 2.6",
   "cards": [
    {
     "tag": "2.2 Mains",
     "q": [
      "What colour is the LIVE wire? What does it do?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "BROWN",
      "→ Carries 230V to appliance",
      "→ Most dangerous wire"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.2 Mains",
     "q": [
      "What colour is the NEUTRAL wire? What does it do?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "BLUE",
      "→ Returns current from appliance",
      "→ At ~0V normally"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.2 Mains",
     "q": [
      "What colour is the EARTH wire? What does it do?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "GREEN / YELLOW",
      "→ Connected to metal casing",
      "→ Safety wire — fault protection"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.2 Mains",
     "q": [
      "What does a FUSE do? Where is it positioned?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "Thin wire that MELTS",
      "if current is too high.",
      "→ Breaks circuit",
      "→ In SERIES with LIVE wire"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.2 Mains",
     "q": [
      "What does DOUBLE INSULATION mean?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "Plastic outer casing —",
      "no metal parts exposed.",
      "→ No earth wire needed",
      "→ Symbol: □ inside □"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.2 Mains",
     "q": [
      "What is the difference between a fuse and an MCB?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "Fuse: melts — must replace",
      "MCB: trips — can reset",
      "MCB is faster to reset"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.6 AC/DC",
     "q": [
      "What is the difference between AC and DC?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "AC: direction reverses repeatedly",
      "→ Mains: 230V, 50Hz",
      "DC: one direction only",
      "→ Cell or battery"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.4 Fuse",
     "q": [
      "FUSE SELECTION: 1150W iron at 230V. Which fuse?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "I = P ÷ V = 1150 ÷ 230 = 5A",
      "Next fuse ABOVE 5A:",
      "→ Use 13A fuse",
      "Available: 3A / 5A / 13A"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Power & Energy",
   "spec": "2.3–2.5",
   "cards": [
    {
     "tag": "2.4 Power",
     "q": [
      "Write the THREE power formulae."
     ],
     "a": [
      "P = I × V",
      "P = I² × R",
      "P = V² ÷ R"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.3 Heating",
     "q": [
      "Why does a current through a resistor cause heating?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "Electrons COLLIDE with",
      "positive metal ions →",
      "energy transferred as heat →",
      "temperature rises"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.3 Heating",
     "q": [
      "Give THREE devices that use the heating effect of electric current."
     ],
     "a": [
      "→ Electric kettle",
      "→ Toaster / Iron",
      "→ Electric heater",
      "→ Filament bulb"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.5 Energy",
     "q": [
      "Write THREE formulae for energy transferred."
     ],
     "a": [
      "E = P × t",
      "E = I × V × t",
      "E = Q × V",
      "(units: J — t in SECONDS)"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.5 Energy",
     "q": [
      "How do you calculate energy cost in kWh?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "Energy (kWh) = kW × hours",
      "Cost = kWh × price/unit",
      "e.g. 2kW × 3h = 6kWh",
      "6kWh × 30fils = 0.18 OMR"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.4 Fuse",
     "q": [
      "2300W shower at 230V. Which fuse? Show working."
     ],
     "a": [
      "I = P/V = 2300/230 = 10A",
      "Next fuse ABOVE 10A:",
      "→ Use 13A fuse"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Ohm's Law & I–V Graphs",
   "spec": "2.8–2.13",
   "cards": [
    {
     "tag": "2.13 V=IR",
     "q": [
      "Write Ohm's Law and its rearrangements."
     ],
     "a": [
      "V = I × R",
      "I = V ÷ R",
      "R = V ÷ I",
      "(V=volts, I=amps, R=ohms)"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.9 I-V",
     "q": [
      "What does the I–V graph for an OHMIC RESISTOR look like?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "Straight line through ORIGIN",
      "→ I is proportional to V",
      "→ Resistance is CONSTANT"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.9 I-V",
     "q": [
      "What does the I–V graph for a FILAMENT LAMP look like?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "Curved line — slope decreases",
      "→ As T rises, R increases",
      "→ NON-OHMIC (not straight)"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.9 I-V",
     "q": [
      "What does the I–V graph for a DIODE look like?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "Almost zero current until",
      "~0.6V threshold, then rises.",
      "→ In reverse: near zero I",
      "→ NON-OHMIC"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.10 Resistance",
     "q": [
      "What happens to current when resistance INCREASES?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "Current DECREASES",
      "I = V ÷ R",
      "(for fixed voltage)",
      "More R → less I"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.13 / 2.19P",
     "q": [
      "Series circuit: total resistance of 20Ω + 40Ω = ?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "R_total = R₁ + R₂",
      "= 20 + 40 = 60 Ω",
      "→ I = V ÷ R_total",
      "(current same everywhere)"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "LDR & Thermistor",
   "spec": "2.11–2.12",
   "cards": [
    {
     "tag": "2.11 LDR",
     "q": [
      "How does LDR resistance change with light?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "IN DARK: resistance HIGH",
      "IN BRIGHT LIGHT: resistance LOW",
      "→ Graph: curve decreasing",
      "→ R ∝ 1/light intensity"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.11 Thermistor",
     "q": [
      "How does thermistor resistance change with temperature?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "COLD: resistance HIGH",
      "HOT: resistance LOW",
      "→ NTC = Negative Temp Coeff.",
      "→ R decreases as T increases"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.11 LDR",
     "q": [
      "Give a real-life use of an LDR."
     ],
     "a": [
      "Automatic street lighting",
      "→ Dark: high R → lamp ON",
      "Also: burglar alarms,",
      "camera exposure meters"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.11 Thermistor",
     "q": [
      "Give a real-life use of a thermistor."
     ],
     "a": [
      "Fire alarm / thermostat",
      "→ Hot: low R → alarm triggers",
      "Also: digital thermometers,",
      "freezer/oven control"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.11 Sensor",
     "q": [
      "LDR in DARK: what happens to Vout in a sensor circuit?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "LDR R HIGH →",
      "total R increases →",
      "current low →",
      "Vout across fixed R is LOW"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Charge, Current & Voltage",
   "spec": "2.14–2.21",
   "cards": [
    {
     "tag": "2.15 Q=It",
     "q": [
      "Write the charge formula and state the units."
     ],
     "a": [
      "Q = I × t",
      "Q = charge (Coulombs, C)",
      "I = current (Amperes, A)",
      "t = time (Seconds, s)"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.16 Electrons",
     "q": [
      "What carries current in a metal wire?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "FREE ELECTRONS (negative)",
      "→ Flow from − to + terminal",
      "→ Opposite to conventional",
      "current direction"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.15 Q=It",
     "q": [
      "4A flows for 3 minutes. Calculate the charge."
     ],
     "a": [
      "t = 3 × 60 = 180 s",
      "Q = It = 4 × 180",
      "Q = 720 C"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.17 Junction",
     "q": [
      "State Kirchhoff's 1st Law."
     ],
     "a": [
      "Current IN = Current OUT",
      "at any junction.",
      "→ Charge is CONSERVED",
      "e.g. 0.6A = 0.4A + 0.2A ✓"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.18 Parallel V",
     "q": [
      "What is the voltage rule for PARALLEL circuits?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "Voltage is the SAME",
      "across ALL branches.",
      "V₁ = V₂ = V_supply",
      "(Spec 2.18)"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.19P Series",
     "q": [
      "12V battery, R₁=20Ω, R₂=40Ω in SERIES. Find I, V₁, V₂."
     ],
     "a": [
      "R_total = 60Ω",
      "I = 12/60 = 0.2A",
      "V₁ = 0.2×20 = 4V",
      "V₂ = 0.2×40 = 8V ✓(4+8=12)"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.20 Voltage",
     "q": [
      "State: what does voltage mean physically?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "Voltage = energy transferred",
      "per unit charge",
      "1 V = 1 J per coulomb",
      "1 V = 1 J/C"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.21 E=QV",
     "q": [
      "E = QV: calculate energy when 50C passes through a 12V component."
     ],
     "a": [
      "E = Q × V",
      "E = 50 × 12",
      "E = 600 J"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Series vs Parallel",
   "spec": "2.7–2.8",
   "cards": [
    {
     "tag": "2.8 Series",
     "q": [
      "State THREE rules for a SERIES circuit."
     ],
     "a": [
      "1. Current SAME everywhere",
      "2. Voltages ADD UP",
      "V₁ + V₂ = V_supply",
      "3. R_total = R₁ + R₂"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.7/2.18 Parallel",
     "q": [
      "State THREE rules for a PARALLEL circuit."
     ],
     "a": [
      "1. Voltage SAME each branch",
      "2. Currents ADD: I=I₁+I₂",
      "3. Total R DECREASES"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.7 Parallel",
     "q": [
      "Why are home lights wired in PARALLEL? (Give 2 reasons)"
     ],
     "a": [
      "1. Each gets full 230V",
      "→ stays at full brightness",
      "2. One failing doesn't",
      "affect the others"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.7 Parallel",
     "q": [
      "What happens when you add a branch to a parallel circuit?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "→ Total R decreases",
      "→ Total current INCREASES",
      "→ Each branch still gets",
      "same voltage"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Resistance of a Wire Practical",
   "spec": "2.14P",
   "cards": [
