     "q": [
      "State FOUR factors that affect wire resistance."
     ],
     "a": [
      "1. Length (R ∝ L)",
      "2. Cross-section area (R∝1/A)",
      "3. Material (resistivity ρ)",
      "4. Temperature"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.14P Practical",
     "q": [
      "Resistance of wire practical: what is the INDEPENDENT variable?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "Length of the wire (L)",
      "→ Varied: 20, 40, 60,",
      "80, 100 cm",
      "→ Dependent: R = V/I"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.14P Practical",
     "q": [
      "Resistance of wire practical: state TWO control variables."
     ],
     "a": [
      "1. Cross-sectional area",
      "of the wire",
      "2. Material of the wire",
      "(also: temperature)"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.14P Practical",
     "q": [
      "What graph do you plot in the wire practical? What shape?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "R (y-axis) vs L (x-axis)",
      "→ Straight line through ORIGIN",
      "→ Confirms: R ∝ L",
      "→ R is directly proportional to L"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Static Electricity",
   "spec": "2.22P–2.28P",
   "cards": [
    {
     "tag": "2.22P Conductors",
     "q": [
      "Name TWO conductors and TWO insulators."
     ],
     "a": [
      "CONDUCTORS:",
      "copper, iron, aluminium",
      "INSULATORS:",
      "plastic, rubber, glass"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.23P / 2.24P",
     "q": [
      "What is transferred when charging by FRICTION?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "ELECTRONS only",
      "→ Protons NEVER move",
      "→ Rubbing SEPARATES charge",
      "→ Total charge stays zero"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.24P Charging",
     "q": [
      "A plastic rod is rubbed. It becomes POSITIVE. Explain why."
     ],
     "a": [
      "Electrons transferred",
      "FROM rod TO cloth.",
      "Rod LOSES electrons →",
      "more protons → POSITIVE"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.25P Forces",
     "q": [
      "State the TWO rules for electrostatic forces."
     ],
     "a": [
      "LIKE charges → REPEL",
      "(++ or −−)",
      "UNLIKE charges → ATTRACT",
      "(+ and −)"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.28P Uses",
     "q": [
      "How does a PHOTOCOPIER use static electricity? (Key principle)"
     ],
     "a": [
      "Drum charged (+) all over →",
      "light removes charge from",
      "white areas → (−) toner",
      "attracted to (+) areas"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.28P Uses",
     "q": [
      "How does an ELECTROSTATIC PRECIPITATOR work?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "Dust gains (−) charge",
      "from mesh wires →",
      "attracted to earthed (+) plates →",
      "dust sticks → clean air out"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.27P Dangers",
     "q": [
      "Why is an aircraft EARTHED before refuelling?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "Aircraft has static charge",
      "from flight →",
      "spark could ignite fuel →",
      "EARTH wire drains charge safely"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.27P Dangers",
     "q": [
      "Name ONE other danger of static electricity and how it is prevented."
     ],
     "a": [
      "Fuel tankers:",
      "charge builds as fuel moves →",
      "metal chain drags on road →",
      "continuous earthing"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Mixed Formula Cards",
   "spec": "All spec",
   "cards": [
    {
     "tag": "2.4 Power",
     "q": [
      "Which power formula to use when you have ONLY I and R?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "P = I² × R",
      "→ Derived from P=IV and V=IR",
      "e.g. I=3A, R=50Ω:",
      "P = 9 × 50 = 450W"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.4 Power",
     "q": [
      "Which power formula to use when you have ONLY V and R?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "P = V² ÷ R",
      "→ Derived from P=IV and I=V/R",
      "e.g. V=230V, R=52.9Ω:",
      "P = 52900/52.9 ≈ 1000W"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.16 Electrons",
     "q": [
      "What is conventional current direction? What about electron flow?"
     ],
     "a": [
      "Conventional: + to −",
      "Electron flow: − to +",
      "(OPPOSITE directions)",
      "Both are accepted in exams"
     ]
    },
    {
     "tag": "2.4 Fuse",
     "q": [
      "How do you select a fuse? State the method."
     ],
     "a": [
      "Step 1: I = P ÷ V",
      "Step 2: Choose NEXT fuse",
      "ABOVE calculated I",
      "Fuses: 3A / 5A / 13A"
     ]
    }
   ]
  }
 ]
}